==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɪˈɡʌn/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-begun.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/bɪˈɡʊn/|a=Northern England,Ireland}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌn|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{head|en|verb form|cat2=irregular past participles}}

# {{inflection of|en|begin||past|part}}
#*: ... when he should take the sield, that city was persuaded to complete the  regiment they had '''begun''' to form, under the command of a Colonel whom the  King had recommended to them ; which they did raise to the number of a  thousand men.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|_|or|_|nonstandard}} {{infl of|en|begin||spast}} {{defdate|17th|20th c.}}

===Adjective===
{{head|en|adj}}

# Having begun.

====Derived terms====
{{col|en|misbegun|unbegun|well begun is half done}}

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|en|a=begnu|Bunge}}

==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*mreǵʰ-}}
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:bref|tree=+}}
From {{inh|en|enm|breef}}, {{m|enm|breve}}, {{m|enm|bref}}, from {{der|en|fro|brief}}, {{m|fro|bref}}, from {{der|en|la|brevis|t=short}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*mréǵʰus|t=short, brief}}. {{doublet|en|breve|merry}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɹiːf/|[ˈbɹʷɪi̯f]|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɹif/|a=US}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-brief.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɾif/|a=Scotland}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɾiːf/|a=Wales}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːf|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|brief}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er,more}}

# Of short [[duration]]; happening quickly. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|temporal}}
#: {{ux|en|Her reign was '''brief''' but spectacular.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|act=III|scene=ii|passage=Some, how '''briefe''' the Life of man / runs his erring pilgrimage, / That the ſtretching of a ſpan, / buckles in his ſumme of age.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=10|passage=It was a joy to snatch some '''brief''' respite, and find himself in the rectory drawing–room. Listening here was as pleasant as talking; just to watch was pleasant. The young priests who lived here wore cassocks and birettas; their faces were fine and mild, yet really strong, like the rector's face; and in their intercourse with him and his wife they seemed to be brothers.}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=November 7, 2012|author=Matt Bai|title=Winning a Second Term, Obama Will Confront Familiar Headwinds|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/us/politics/in-president-obamas-second-term-familiar-challenges.html
|passage=That '''brief''' moment after the election four years ago, when many Americans thought Mr. Obama’s election would presage a new, less fractious political era, now seems very much a thing of the past.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20200603203209/https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/us/politics/in-president-obamas-second-term-familiar-challenges.html}}
# [[concise|Concise]]; taking few words. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|His speech of acceptance was '''brief''' but moving.}}
#* {{RQ:Travers Cuckoo in the Nest|chapter=1|passage=She was like a Beardsley ''Salome'', he had said. And indeed she had the narrow eyes and the high cheekbone of that creature, and as nearly the sinuosity as is compatible with human symmetry. His wooing had been '''brief''' but incisive.}}
# Occupying a small distance, area or [[spatial]] extent; [[short]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author=Robert Drewe|title=The Bodysurfers|page=17|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2009
|passage=On the beach he always wore a straw hat with a red band and a '''brief''' pair of leopard print trunks.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Rife; common; prevalent.

====Synonyms====
* See also [[Thesaurus:ephemeral]]
* See also [[Thesaurus:concise]]

